---
<Base
  title="Measuring a GUI app without lying to yourself — LightPhotos Blog"
  desc="You cannot measure the speed of a desktop app by clicking through it, because you never do the same thing twice. This post shows the small tool that I built instead, and the three reports that changed what I worked on."
  active="blogs"
>
  <main class="lp-page">
    <article class="lp-article">
      <header class="lp-article-head">
        <p class="lp-article-meta"><a href="/blogs.html">Blogs</a> &middot; <time datetime="2026-09-15">September 15, 2026</time></p>
        <h1>Measuring a GUI app without lying to yourself</h1>
        <p class="lp-article-dek">
          You cannot measure the speed of a desktop app by clicking through it,
          because you never do the same thing twice. This post shows the small
          tool that I built instead, and the three reports that changed what I
          worked on.
        </p>
        <p class="lp-article-byline">Andrew Yao &middot; 5 min read</p>
      </header>

      <aside class="lp-tldr">
        <p class="lp-tldr-label">TL;DR</p>
        <p>
          I spent a long time making the thumbnail grid faster, but it was
          already fast. The real wait came when the app opened a RAW file. So I
          added a mode that runs the same fixed script on a folder and prints
          timings, without a window. Its first three reports showed me that I
          worked on the wrong things. If you tune an app by your impressions, a
          repeatable measurement will probably surprise you too.
        </p>
      </aside>

      <figure class="lp-figure">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 198" role="img" aria-label="Top: clicking through the app by hand is crossed out, because I cannot do the same thing twice. Below: the --profile flag runs a pipeline. A fixed script of 120 thumbnails and 10 photos goes to the real app code with no window and no graphics card. That code prints timings that give the same number on every run. The timings make a report that shows where the wait really is.">
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="12">HOW I MEASURE THE APP</text>

          <rect class="lp-fig-bad-stroke" x="0" y="28" width="300" height="40" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="16" y="53">Click through the app by hand</text>
          <path class="lp-fig-bad-stroke" stroke-width="2" d="M274 42 L286 54 M286 42 L274 54"></path>
          <text class="lp-fig-s lp-fig-bad" x="316" y="53">I cannot do the same thing twice</text>

          <text class="lp-fig-t lp-fig-mono" x="0" y="100">--profile &lt;folder&gt;</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="150" y="100">one command, then the app exits</text>

          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="0" y="114" width="142" height="72" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="12" y="138">Fixed script</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="12" y="157">120 thumbnails</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="12" y="173">then 10 photos</text>

          <path class="lp-fig-line" d="M145 150 H155"></path>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M155 145 L163 150 L155 155 Z"></path>

          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="166" y="114" width="142" height="72" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="178" y="138">Real app code</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="178" y="157">no window</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="178" y="173">no graphics card</text>

          <path class="lp-fig-line" d="M311 150 H321"></path>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M321 145 L329 150 L321 155 Z"></path>

          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="332" y="114" width="142" height="72" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="344" y="138">Timings</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="344" y="157">the same number</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="344" y="173">on every run</text>

          <path class="lp-fig-line" d="M477 150 H487"></path>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M487 145 L495 150 L487 155 Z"></path>

          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="498" y="114" width="142" height="72" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="510" y="138">Report</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="510" y="157">shows where the</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="510" y="173">wait really is</text>
        </svg>
        <figcaption>
          The script takes me out of the measurement. The same steps run every
          time, so a change in the number comes from a change in the code.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2>What I learned</h2>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>A benchmark has to give the same answer twice.</strong> A
          realistic test can give a different number on every run. Such a test
          cannot tell me if a change helped, because I have no stable value to
          compare against.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Take yourself out of the measurement.</strong> If a
          measurement needs me to operate the app, I script the steps instead.
          I do this even if the script is less realistic than a real session.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Write down why the test does what it does.</strong> "120
          thumbnails, because that is two screenfuls" is a reason that someone
          can question. "120" alone is only a number that nobody understands.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Measure memory as well as time.</strong> One function caused
          47% of the memory that the app asked for. None of my timing reports
          showed it. It mattered most in the browser version, which has the
          least memory.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        I spent a long time making the thumbnail grid faster. It was already
        fast, and nobody waited for it.
      </p>
      <p>
        People actually waited when they opened a RAW file. I never measured
        that. A correct measurement was inconvenient, and I had a guess about
        where the time went. My guess was wrong.
      </p>

      <h2>Why I could not just use the app</h2>
      <p>
        In the usual method, you attach a profiler (a tool that records where
        the program spends its time). Then you click through the app for thirty
        seconds and read the chart that the profiler makes. That is fine for
        finding something that is badly broken. It did not help me decide if a
        change made the app better, and that is the question I care about.
      </p>
      <p>
        The problem is that I cannot do the same thing twice. On the first run,
        I scrolled a bit fast and reached row 40. On the second run, I reached
        row 31. On the first run, the thumbnail cache was empty. On the second
        run, it was full, because the first run filled it.
      </p>

      <figure class="lp-figure">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 186" role="img" aria-label="Dot plot: six manual runs have a wide spread on the time axis. Six scripted profile runs have almost the same value.">
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="12">SIX RUNS OF THE SAME &ldquo;BENCHMARK&rdquo;</text>

          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="0" y="52">Clicks by hand</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="70">in the app</text>
          <line class="lp-fig-line-soft" x1="176" y1="56" x2="624" y2="56"></line>
          <circle class="lp-fig-bad" cx="192" cy="56" r="6"></circle>
          <circle class="lp-fig-bad" cx="268" cy="56" r="6"></circle>
          <circle class="lp-fig-bad" cx="341" cy="56" r="6"></circle>
          <circle class="lp-fig-bad" cx="433" cy="56" r="6"></circle>
          <circle class="lp-fig-bad" cx="521" cy="56" r="6"></circle>
          <circle class="lp-fig-bad" cx="598" cy="56" r="6"></circle>
          <path class="lp-fig-bad-stroke" d="M192 76 V84 M192 80 H598 M598 76 V84"></path>
          <text class="lp-fig-s lp-fig-bad" x="395" y="102" text-anchor="middle">the spread comes from you</text>

          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="0" y="136">--profile</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="154">the same script, always</text>
          <line class="lp-fig-line-soft" x1="176" y1="140" x2="624" y2="140"></line>
          <circle class="lp-fig-bar" cx="392" cy="140" r="6"></circle>
          <circle class="lp-fig-bar" cx="399" cy="140" r="6"></circle>
          <circle class="lp-fig-bar" cx="404" cy="140" r="6"></circle>
          <circle class="lp-fig-bar" cx="397" cy="140" r="6"></circle>
          <circle class="lp-fig-bar" cx="401" cy="140" r="6"></circle>
          <circle class="lp-fig-bar" cx="395" cy="140" r="6"></circle>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="398" y="172" text-anchor="middle">a change that you can see</text>
        </svg>
        <figcaption>
          My before and after numbers came from different sessions. I could
          find almost any meaning in that data, and that is what I did.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2>Run the code without the window</h2>
      <p>
        My fix was a command-line flag. <code>--profile &lt;folder&gt;</code>
        starts the app and runs the real code on that folder. This code lists
        the folder and loads photos, the catalog, and thumbnails. Then the app
        prints timings and exits. It never opens a window, so it does not use
        the UI library or the graphics card.
      </p>
      <p>
        It follows a fixed script. First, it lists the folder and fills
        thumbnails for 120 photos, which is approximately two screenfuls. Then
        it opens ten photos in sequence, as you do when you step through a
        shoot with the arrow keys. Each of those numbers has a comment in the
        code that tells why. The numbers are my guess about how people use the
        app, so I want that guess written next to the number.
      </p>
      <p>
        The full mode is approximately 230 lines. It is not clever. It only
        gives me the same number twice.
      </p>

      <h2>Keep it out of the version that you download</h2>
      <p>
        Small markers attach the timing code to functions. Only two build
        settings switch it on, and they are off by default. When these settings
        are off, the markers do not change the function. So the release build
        contains none of the timing code, and the browser build does not get
        any bigger.
      </p>
      <p>
        One detail surprised me. The profiling library has to be a normal
        dependency, not an optional one. The reason is that the compiler must
        understand the markers in every build, even when they do nothing.
      </p>

      <h2>The first report</h2>

      <figure class="lp-figure">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 196" role="img" aria-label="Bar chart: the folder list takes 3 to 5 milliseconds. 120 warm thumbnails take 22 milliseconds. A RAW preview takes 11 milliseconds. A sharp RAW decode takes 170 to 210 milliseconds.">
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="12">300 PHOTOS, WARM CACHE</text>

          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="51">List the folder</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bar-dim" x="226" y="34" width="9" height="24" rx="2"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="245" y="51">3 to 5 ms</text>

          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="91">Fill 120 thumbnails</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bar-dim" x="226" y="74" width="46" height="24" rx="2"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="282" y="91">22 ms</text>

          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="131">Open a RAW, preview</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bar-dim" x="226" y="114" width="23" height="24" rx="2"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="259" y="131">11 ms</text>

          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="0" y="171">Open a RAW, sharp</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bar" x="226" y="154" width="400" height="24" rx="2"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-on-bar" x="238" y="171">170 to 210 ms &middot; all of the wait is here</text>
        </svg>
        <figcaption>
          Almost everything on this chart was already fast enough. So I could
          stop working on it.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        I made the wrong thing faster, and the report showed me this in
        approximately four seconds.
      </p>
      <p>
        The report also showed a fix that I would never find by reading the
        code. The app fetches the quick, low-quality preview of the next photo
        ahead of time. But it does not fetch the slow, sharp version. So when
        you use the arrow keys in a folder of RAW files, you wait the full
        200 ms every time. This occurs although the app knew half a second
        earlier which photo you would look at next.
      </p>

      <h2>The second report</h2>
      <p>
        The profiler can also count how much memory each function asks for,
        not only how long it takes. One function asked for 12.0 MB out of
        25.3 MB. That is 47% of all the memory that the app asked for in that
        session. It was the function that gives thumbnails to the UI library
        to draw.
      </p>
      <p>
        The cause was the way that the UI library accepts images. You can only
        make its image type if you copy the pixels into new memory. But the
        pixels were already in the cache of the loader, in exactly the right
        layout. So I wrote them directly to the graphics card instead. That
        change took the function from 12.0 MB to 0.16 MB, and the full session
        from 25.3 MB to 13.4 MB.
      </p>
      <p>
        Nothing was slow, and no user would ever report it. But in the browser
        build, the app runs as WebAssembly, and its memory never decreases
        after it increases. So memory that the app needs for only a moment
        stays in use while the tab is open.
      </p>

      <h2>The third report</h2>
      <p>
        I used 200 photos with a warm cache. The decode of all 200 thumbnails
        on 8 workers took 39.5 ms in total. The analysis of one photo took
        7.39 ms, so 1.48 seconds for the batch. The analysis runs on the main
        thread, and it cannot be split across the processor cores. So it costs
        approximately fifty-five times as much as the decode.
      </p>
      <p>
        My plan was to make the decode faster and to fetch it all ahead of
        time. Neither change would make a difference that you can notice.
      </p>



